.TH M4 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
m4 \- macro processor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B m4
.RB [ \-s ]
.RI [ \-D " name" [ = value ]]...
.RI [ \-U " name" ]...
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B m4
copies each
.I file
(or standard input, with no
.I file
operands) to standard output, expanding every macro call it
recognizes as it goes. A macro's expansion is itself rescanned for
further macro calls before any following text is read, so a call may
expand into another call and so on; arguments are collected the same
way, so they are expanded as they are collected unless quoted.
.PP
Text is quoted by default with a leading
.B \(ga
and a trailing
.RB \' ,
which may be changed with
.BR changequote .
Quoted text is copied through with the delimiters stripped and is
never itself scanned for macro names, so quoting a macro name's own
arguments is the standard way to prevent premature expansion.
.RB \(lq # \(rq
begins a comment that runs to the next newline by default (changeable
with
.BR changecom ).
A comment's text is copied through verbatim and is never scanned for
macro calls either.
.SH BUILT-IN MACROS
All thirty-two of the macros required by the POSIX
.B m4
specification are implemented:
.BR define ", " undefine ", " defn ", " pushdef ", " popdef ","
.BR ifdef ", " ifelse ", " shift ", " dnl ", " changequote ","
.BR changecom ", " include ", " sinclude ", " divert ", " undivert ","
.BR divnum ", " len ", " index ", " substr ", " translit ","
.BR dumpdef ", " eval ", " incr ", " decr ", " syscmd ", " sysval ","
.BR maketemp ", " mkstemp ", " m4exit ", " m4wrap ", " traceon ","
and
.BR traceoff .
No macro outside this set is built in: a name this implementation does
not define as a builtin, and that the running script has not defined
itself, is never a macro call at all \(em it is copied through as
plain text, the same as any other undefined identifier. In
particular, GNU extensions such as
.BR __file__ ", " __line__ ", " errprint ", " esyscmd ", " format ","
.BR patsubst ", " regexp ", " indir ", " builtin ","
and
.BR undivert 's
filename-argument form, are not implemented; using one of those names
where no such macro has been user-defined leaves the name in the
output literally.
.PP
This implementation's own behavior for several macros where the
standard is silent or where a common extension exists:
.TP
.B shift
Its output is each remaining argument individually re-quoted with the
current quote strings (the same as
.BR $@ ),
not a bare comma-joined list.
.TP
.B changequote
With no arguments, resets quoting to the default
.B \(ga
/
.RB \' .
Given exactly one argument, or an argument that is an empty string,
is refused with a diagnostic and leaves quoting unchanged \(em the
standard leaves this case unspecified, and this implementation
declines to guess rather than pick an arbitrary reading. Each
delimiter string is limited to 32 bytes; longer is refused with a
diagnostic.
.TP
.B changecom
With no arguments, or an empty first argument, turns comment
recognition off. With one non-empty argument, sets the start delimiter
and defaults the end delimiter to a newline. Each delimiter string is
limited to 32 bytes; longer is refused with a diagnostic.
.TP
.BR divert / undivert
Nine numbered diversion buffers (1\(en9) are supported, held
in memory rather than in temporary files; diversion 0 is real standard
output. Diverting to a negative number discards output; diverting to
a number greater than 9 is refused with a diagnostic and leaves the
current diversion unchanged.
.B undivert
with no arguments empties buffers 1 through 9, in numeric order, into
whichever diversion is currently active; given arguments, each must
be a plain diversion number (the GNU filename form is not
implemented). Any diversion still holding data when the input is
exhausted is flushed to real standard output regardless of which
diversion is current at that point, including after an
.B m4exit
call.
.TP
.B eval
Evaluates its first argument as an integer expression using signed
32-bit arithmetic; overflow wraps around modulo 2^32, and
division and modulus truncate toward zero. An optional second
argument sets the output radix (2\(en36, default 10) and an optional
third sets a minimum field width, zero-padded. A negative result is
rendered as a
.RB \(lq \- \(rq
followed by the magnitude's digits; radix digits above 9 use
lowercase letters.
.TP
.B translit
Maps each byte of the source string found in the second
.RI ( from )
argument to the byte at the same index in the third
.RI ( to )
argument, deleting it if
.I to
is shorter than
.IR from ;
a
.RB \(lq \- \(rq
in either argument has no special range meaning.
.TP
.B syscmd
Runs its argument as a shell command; the command's own output goes
directly to this process's existing standard output/error, not
captured into
.BR syscmd 's
expansion (which is always the empty string). The command's exit
status is recorded for a following
.B sysval
call (128 plus the signal number if the command was killed by a
signal).
.TP
.BR maketemp / mkstemp
.B mkstemp
replaces a trailing run of
.RB \(lq X \(rq
characters in its argument with characters chosen to name a newly
created, real, collision-free temporary file, and expands to the
resulting pathname.
.B maketemp
is the older, non-collision-safe form: its trailing
.RB \(lq X \(rq
run is simply replaced with this process's decimal process ID.
.TP
.B m4exit
Arranges for processing to stop and this process to exit once the
current point in the input is reached, with the given exit code (or,
if no argument is given, 1 if any error has already occurred during
this run, else 0).
.TP
.B m4wrap
Queues its argument's text to be processed, in the order queued, after
the real end of input is reached (a call to
.B m4wrap
while processing already-queued text appends further text, which is
likewise processed before the run ends).
.TP
.BR traceon / traceoff
With no arguments, turns tracing of every macro call on or off; given
one or more names, turns tracing on or off for just those names. A
traced call prints
\(lqm4trace: \fIname\fR(\fIargs\fR)\en\(rq
to standard error.
.PP
Definitions made with
.B pushdef
stack; a later
.B popdef
or a matching
.B undefine
uncovers the previous definition (\fBundefine\fR removes the entire
stack for a name at once, not just its top).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-D name [ = value ]
Defines
.I name
as if by
\(lqdefine(\fIname\fR,\fIvalue\fR)\(rq
before any input is read;
.I value
defaults to the empty string if
\(lq=\fIvalue\fR\(rq
is omitted.
.TP
.BI \-U name
Undefines
.I name
as if by
\(lqundefine(\fIname\fR)\(rq
before any input is read.
.IP
.BR \-D " and " \-U
are applied strictly in the left-to-right order they appear on the
command line, interleaved with each other, so a later option always
wins over an earlier one naming the same macro.
.TP
.B \-s
Accepted for compatibility and otherwise has no effect: this
implementation never produces line-synchronization output for a
downstream preprocessor.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an input file, read and processed in the order given.
.RB \(lq \- \(rq
means standard input. With no
.I file
operands, standard input is read.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
A pathological input such as
.RI \(lq define(a,a)a \(rq
expands forever by design, the same as any other implementation of
.BR m4 ;
nothing in this build changes that. What this build does bound,
because it can run in-process as a shell built-in with no separate
process to interrupt, is runaway recursion from ever corrupting the
process itself: a cap of two million total macro invocations, and a
separate cap of 5000 levels of real call nesting (relevant to a shape
like
.RI \(lq len(len(len(...))) \(rq
rather than a long flat chain), each independently aborting the run
with a diagnostic and a nonzero exit if reached. Neither limit is
reachable by an ordinarily-written script.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Processing completed with no error and no
.B m4exit
call, or
.B m4exit
was called with an argument of 0.
.TP
.I other
An
.B m4exit
call with a nonzero argument sets that value as the exit status. Absent an explicit
.BR m4exit ,
an exit status of 1 indicates an error occurred during the run (a
malformed option, an unreadable input or
.B include
file, an invalid
.B eval
expression, or a runaway-expansion limit reached).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR awk (1),
.BR patch (1)
